{"records":[{"id":"ztg7pxr6ch","title":"STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency","description":"This dataset contains the original raw and analyzed data supporting the manuscript “STT3A is essential for Wnt signaling and represents a target for cancers driven by RNF43 deficiency”. The study identifies STT3A as an essential regulator of the Wnt signaling pathway and a potential therapeutic target for RNF43-deficient cancers. Included data were generated from key experiments, including Wnt reporter assays (DDT), Western blotting (WB), and CRISPR screen, and other experiments central to the study","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-10-13T11:26:58.874Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ztg7pxr6ch","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ztg7pxr6ch"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Zhengjin","last_name":"He","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"f7btcr6x6d","title":"A non-canonical function of glutathione to suppress ferroptosis via FSP1","description":"Whole genome-wide CRISPR KO screen","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-10-02T11:41:52.925Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/f7btcr6x6d","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/f7btcr6x6d"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"BO","last_name":"CHU","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Cell Biology"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"y8dndzn3mg","title":"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication","description":"These data are part of the Supplemental Items associated with the manuscript entitled \"Genome-scale CRISPR screen identifies TMEM41B as a critical host factor required for coronavirus replication\". These files include Supplementary Data file 1-4.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-03-16T10:34:13.579Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/y8dndzn3mg","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/y8dndzn3mg"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Limeng","last_name":"Sun","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Virus","Screening","CRISPR/Cas9"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"8xhv88c7wt","title":"Genome-wide screening in human kidney organoids identifies developmental and disease-related aspects of nephrogenesis","description":"Human organoids allow the study of proliferation, lineage specification, and three-dimensional tissue development. Here, we present a genome-wide CRISPR screen in iPSC-derived kidney organoids. The combination of inducible genome editing, longitudinal sampling and endpoint sorting of tubular and stromal cells generated a complex, high-quality dataset uncovering a broad spectrum of insightful biology from early development to ‘adult’ epithelial morphogenesis. Our functional dataset allows improving mesoderm induction by ROCK inhibition, contains monogenetic and complex trait kidney disease genes, confirms two additional CAKUT genes (CCDC170 and MYH7B), and provides a large candidate list of ciliopathy-related genes. Finally, the identification of a cis-inhibitory effect of Jagged1 controlling epithelial proliferation shows how mosaic knockouts in pooled CRISPR screening can discover ways of communication between heterogeneous cell populations in complex tissues. Collectively, these data serve both as a rich resource for the kidney community and as a benchmark for future iPSC-derived organoid CRISPR screens.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2021-11-29T13:48:59.335Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/8xhv88c7wt","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/8xhv88c7wt"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Rosemarie","last_name":"Ungricht","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Philipp","last_name":"Hoppe","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Screening","CRISPR"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"zhpg8xshp9","title":"Lawrence et al., PNAS 2026","description":"Data from “Lawrence et al., 2026 A Genome-Wide Screen Identifies that PLCG2 Restrains Lysosomal GCase Activity.\"","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2026-06-22T18:23:16.389Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/zhpg8xshp9","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/zhpg8xshp9"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Baris","last_name":"Bingol","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Natural Sciences"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"fm49hhpdg3","title":"Separation-of-function mutants reveal the NF-kB-independent involvement of IkBα in the regulation of intestinal stemness","description":"We have previously shown that the NF-κB inhibitor IkBa binds the chromatin together with PRC2 to confer responsiveness to PRC2 targets in the presence of inflammatory cues. This alternative function has been elusive in both physiological and disease conditions because of the predominant role of IkBa as a negative regulator of NF-κB. Here, we uniquely characterize the specific residues of IkBa that allow the generation of separation-of-function (SOF) mutants that are defective for either NF-κB-related (SOF NF-κB) or chromatin-related (SOF H2A,H4) activities. Expression of IkBa SOF NF-κB, but not SOF H2A/H4, is sufficient to negatively regulate a specific stemness program in intestinal cells, thereby rescuing the differentiation block imposed by IkBa deficiency. By ChIP assay, we demonstrated that IkBa binds to several stemness genes that are transcriptionally repressed upon IkBa SOF NF-κB induction. Our data suggest that SOF mutants provide an exclusive tool for studying IkBa functions in physiology and disease.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2025-05-20T12:20:10.739Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/fm49hhpdg3","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/fm49hhpdg3"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Daniel","last_name":"Álvarez-Villanueva","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Martin","last_name":"Floor","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Solé Font","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Luis","last_name":"G Palma","orcid":null},{"first_name":"María","last_name":"Maqueda","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Joan","last_name":"Bertran","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Teresa","last_name":"Lobo-Jarne","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Rajani","last_name":"Rajbhandari","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Aran","last_name":"Merati","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Laura","last_name":"Marruecos","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Jordi","last_name":"Villà-Freixa","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Mar","last_name":"Iglesias","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Markus","last_name":"Bredel","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Anna","last_name":"Bigas","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Lluis","last_name":"Espinosa","orcid":null}],"funders":[{"id":"https://ror.org/00ca2c886","name":"Instituto de Salud Carlos III","grant_id":"PI22/00069 "},{"id":"https://ror.org/00ca2c886","name":"Instituto de Salud Carlos III","grant_id":"DTS23/00005"}],"categories":["Western Blot"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nf3hm5dj9m","title":"ALI data","description":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) is one of the neurotransmitters released by nonadrenergic noncholinergic inhibitory nerve endings. In lung tissues, VIP can inhibit alveolar macrophage phagocytosis and T lymphocyte proliferation, reduce the release of inflammatory mediators, and promote the repair of airway epithelial damage, thus playing an important role in treating pulmonary systemic diseases. This study aimed to explore the effects of VIP on lipopolysaccharide (LPS)-induced macrophage activation in vitro and LPS-induced acute lung injury (ALI) in mice. Mouse primary alveolar macrophage cells were treated with LPS (100 ng/mL) for 8 h and then co-incubated with different concentrations of VIP (108–106 mol/L) for 24 h. VIP treatment significantly inhibited LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and decreased the activation of the nuclear transcription factor-κB (NF-κB) signaling pathway. Subsequent studies confirmed that VIP-inhibited alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were dependent on the activation of Adenosine cyclic 3', 5'-monophosphate(cAMP)-protein kinase A(PKA)pathway. ALI was induced in mice by intraperitoneal (ip) injection of LPS (10 mg/kg), and different doses of VIP (1, 3, and 5 nmol/mouse, ip) were administered at 0, 8, and 16 h after LPS injection. VIP significantly reduced LPS-induced inflammatory cell infiltration, decreased the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-α, IL-1β, and IL-6), and eliminated LPS-mediated pulmonary edema. Also, LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were inhibited. In conclusion, VIP could effectively reduce the LPS-induced inflammatory responses in vitro and in vivo; this beneficial effect was associated with the activation of cAMP-PKA pathway and the inhibition of NF-κB signaling pathway activation, thereby reducing the expression of pro-inflammatory mediators and cytokines. These data suggest that VIP may be a potential drug for the treatment of ALI.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-31T06:29:14.599Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nf3hm5dj9m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nf3hm5dj9m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Jingtao","last_name":"LI","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Respiratory System"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"ss6ghkd8bk","title":"Inhibition of M1 polarization in alveolar macrophages by vasoactive intestinal peptide attenuates LPS-induced acute lung injury in vivo and vitro","description":"Vasoactive intestinal peptide (VIP) is one of the neurotransmitters released by nonadrenergic noncholinergic inhibitory nerve endings. In lung tissues, VIP can inhibit alveolar macrophage phagocytosis and T lymphocyte proliferation, reduce the release of inflammatory mediators, and promote the repair of airway epithelial damage, thus playing an important role in treating pulmonary systemic diseases. This study aimed to explore the effects of VIP on lipopolysaccharide (LPS)-induced macrophage activation in vitro and LPS-induced acute lung injury (ALI) in mice. Mouse primary alveolar macrophage cells were treated with LPS (100 ng/mL) for 8 h and then co-incubated with different concentrations of VIP (108–106 mol/L) for 24 h. VIP treatment significantly inhibited LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and decreased the activation of the nuclear transcription factor-κB (NF-κB) signaling pathway. Subsequent studies confirmed that VIP-inhibited alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were dependent on the activation of Adenosine cyclic 3', 5'-monophosphate(cAMP)-protein kinase A(PKA)pathway. ALI was induced in mice by intraperitoneal (ip) injection of LPS (10 mg/kg), and different doses of VIP (1, 3, and 5 nmol/mouse, ip) were administered at 0, 8, and 16 h after LPS injection. VIP significantly reduced LPS-induced inflammatory cell infiltration, decreased the production of pro-inflammatory cytokines (TNF-α, IL-1β, and IL-6), and eliminated LPS-mediated pulmonary edema. Also, LPS-induced alveolar macrophage M1 polarization and NF-κB signaling pathway activation were inhibited. In conclusion, VIP could effectively reduce the LPS-induced inflammatory responses in vitro and in vivo; this beneficial effect was associated with the activation of cAMP-PKA pathway and the inhibition of NF-κB signaling pathway activation, thereby reducing the expression of pro-inflammatory mediators and cytokines. These data suggest that VIP may be a potential drug for the treatment of ALI.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2022-10-31T06:29:35.873Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/ss6ghkd8bk","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/ss6ghkd8bk"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"honggang","last_name":"wang","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Lung Injury"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"65pfcj4j4m","title":"Protocol of CRISPR-Cas9 knockout screens for identifying ferroptosis regulators","description":"Ferroptosis, an iron-dependent programmed cell death triggered by excessive lipid peroxidation, has shown promising therapeutic potentials in human diseases. Here, we describe a protocol of a CRISPR-Cas9 loss-of-function screen to identify regulators in response to different inducers of ferroptosis. We emphasize the steps of library amplification, drug treatment, high-throughput sequencing preparation, and bioinformatics analysis using Model-based Analysis of Genome-wide CRISPR-Cas9 Knockout (MAGeCK). We also present a method to discover the regulators of ferroptosis and verify the potential targets efficiently.","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2023-10-24T01:46:48.793Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/65pfcj4j4m","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/65pfcj4j4m"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Xin","last_name":"Yang","orcid":null},{"first_name":"Wei","last_name":"Gu","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["CRISPR/Cas9","Ferroptosis"],"journal_issn":[]},{"id":"nxvdzybwhf","title":"Illuminating host-mycobacterial interactions with genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screens","description":"The data deposited here contains genome-wide CRISPR knockout and CRISPRi screen data, including the positive hits, the negative hits, and non-targeting controls. ","source":{"id":"7377952c-6404-4428-bbfc-77242cfc301e","name":"Mendeley Data","external":false},"publication_date":"2020-08-30T11:32:01.108Z","url":"https://data.mendeley.com/datasets/nxvdzybwhf","data_type":"dataset","access":"open","doi":["10.17632/nxvdzybwhf"],"contributors":[{"first_name":"Yong","last_name":"Lai","orcid":null}],"funders":[],"categories":["Functional Genomics","High-Throughput Sequencing","Tuberculosis"],"journal_issn":[]}],"total_records":131117,"page":1,"page_size":10,"execution_time":170,"aggregations":{"data_type_stats":{"dataset":131117,"collection":0},"access_type_stats":{"open":130566,"restricted":64,"embargoed":487},"top_funders_stats":[{"id":"https://ror.org/01h0zpd94","name":"National Natural Science Foundation of China","count":2183},{"id":"https://ror.org/003x0zc53","name":"Agencia Estatal de Investigación","count":1429},{"id":"https://ror.org/01cwqze88","name":"National Institutes of Health","count":609},{"id":"https://ror.org/03ha2q922","name":"National Science Center","count":454},{"id":"https://ror.org/02ddkpn78","name":"Fundação de Amparo à Pesquisa do Estado de São Paulo","count":440},{"id":"https://ror.org/00x0ma614","name":"Coordenação de Aperfeicoamento de Pessoal de Nível Superior","count":416},{"id":"https://ror.org/03swz6y49","name":"Conselho Nacional de Desenvolvimento Científico e Tecnológico","count":409},{"id":"https://ror.org/021nxhr62","name":"U.S. National Science Foundation","count":364},{"id":"https://ror.org/018mejw64","name":"Deutsche Forschungsgemeinschaft","count":291},{"id":"https://ror.org/00hhkn466","name":"Japan Society for the Promotion of Science","count":259}],"source_stats":[{"id":"MENDELEY_DATA","name":"Mendeley Data","count":128508},{"id":"f881433a-b1cd-40d2-ab9f-4fd501f2d14b","name":"Elsevier Data Repository","count":1900},{"id":"a191461c-0227-403f-a1e9-621e0e0429cc","name":"San Raffaele Open Research Data Repository","count":181},{"id":"b07018a5-fee6-4df1-9d43-ff0d81ad